==English==

===Alternative forms===
* [[msg]]

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*mey- (change)}}
From {{inh|en|enm|message}}, from {{der|en|fro|message}}, from {{der|en|la-eme|missāticum}}, derived from {{der|en|la|mitto|mittere|t=send}}, ultimately from {{der|en|ine-pro|*meyth₂-|t=to exchange}}. Partially {{displaced|ang|ærende|nocap=1}} (whence {{m|en|errand}}).

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈmɛs.ɪdʒ/|a=RP}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈmɛs.ɪdʒ/|/ˈmɛs.ədʒ/<a:weak vowel>|a=GA}}
** {{audio|en|en-us-message.ogg|a=US}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈmes.ədʒ/|a=AU,NZ}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɛsɪdʒ|s=2}}
* {{hyph|en|mes|sage}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[communication]], or what is [[communicate]]d; any concept or information conveyed. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
#: {{ux|en|We've just received an urgent '''message''' from the President.}}
#: {{ux|en| He received a '''message''' telling him to update his laptop.}}
#* {{RQ:KJV|Judges|3|20|passage=I have a '''message''' from God unto thee.}}
#* {{RQ:Churchill Celebrity|chapter=IV|page=46|passage=No matter how early I came down, I would find him on the veranda, smoking cigarettes, or otherwise his man would be there with a '''message''' to say that his master would shortly join me if I would kindly wait.}}
# An [[errand]]. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
# {{lb|en|Ireland|Scotland|NZ|Caribbean|chiefly|in plural}} An errand to a shop, a shopping expedition; an item of shopping; {{i|in plural}} [[groceries]]. {{defdate|from 18th c.}}
#* {{RQ:Joyce Portrait|chapter=II||On week days he did '''messages''' between the house in Carysfort Avenue and those shops in the main street of the town with which the family dealt.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1952|author={{w|Sam Selvon}}|title=A Brighter Sun
|passage=Hurry up, man. Look other people in the shop who want '''message'''.}}
# An [[underlying]] [[theme]] or [[conclusion]] to be drawn from something. {{defdate|from 19th c.}}
#: {{ux|en|The main '''message''' of the novel is that time heals all wounds.}}
#* {{RQ:Economist|date=2013-06-29|volume=407|issue=8842|page=55
|url=http://www.economist.com/news/international/21580191-edward-snowdens-odyssey-leaves-america-nonplussed-and-its-allies-dismayed-russia-china|title=Travels and travails
|passage=But, as with Hollywood, the subplots and exotic locations may distract from the real '''message''': America’s discomfort and its foes’ glee.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20171125151142/http://www.economist.com:80/news/international/21580191-edward-snowdens-odyssey-leaves-america-nonplussed-and-its-allies-dismayed-russia-china|archivedate=25 November 2017}}

